truetypeshaping.library/ShapeGlyphs        truetypeshaping.library/ShapeGlyphs

   NAME
	ShapeGlyphs -- A run of glyphs shaped by a font's layout tables: the glyphs replaced, and each given its advance and its offsets.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn ShapeGlyphs(sb: *TrueTypeShapingBase, outline: *truetype.Outline, script: u32, direction: u32, language: u32, features: ?[*]const truetypeshaping.Feature, run: *truetypeshaping.ShapedRun) i32

   SINCE
	1.0, a variable font at its instance since 1.3. LVO -20.

   INPUTS
	- `outline` - what truetype.library's `OpenOutline` answered.
	- `script` - the script's four letters, as `scriptTag` makes them
	  (`scriptTag("latn")`), or `TTS_SCRIPT_DEFAULT`.
	- `direction` - `TTS_LEFT_TO_RIGHT` or `TTS_RIGHT_TO_LEFT`: the way
	  the run is read.
	- `language` - the language's four letters (`languageTag("TRK ")`), or
	  `TTS_LANGUAGE_DEFAULT`.
	- `features` - the features to apply, a list ended by a tag of 0;
	  null for none.
	- `run` - the glyphs: `count` of them in `glyphs`, each with its
	  `glyph` and its `cluster` set, in the order they are read; `room`
	  says how many `glyphs` holds.

   RESULT
	`TTS_OK`, and `run` holds the shaped glyphs in the order they are
	drawn, from left to right, `count` of them. Otherwise:
	- `TTSERR_ROOM` - the shaped run has more glyphs than `room`: `count`
	  says how many, and `glyphs` is as it was.
	- `TTSERR_NOMEM` - there was not the memory.
	- `TTSERR_DIRECTION` - no direction of that number.
	- `TTSERR_FEATURES` - more than 31 features that hold for a part of
	  the run or whose tag the list has twice.
	But for `TTSERR_ROOM`, `run` is as it was.

   BEHAVIOR
	The font's `GDEF`, `GSUB` and `GPOS` are read through `FontTable`. In
	each of the two the script is looked for - then `DFLT`, `dflt` and
	`latn` - and in the script the language, then the language `dflt`,
	then the script's default. What the language requires is applied to
	every glyph. Of the features asked for, those the language lists are
	applied; the others do nothing.

	A feature holds for the glyphs whose cluster is within its `first`
	and `last`. Where two of one tag hold for a glyph, the later one of
	the list does, so a feature is switched off for a part of the run by
	a second entry of the value 0. Where a feature offers several forms
	of a glyph, its value picks one, counted from 1.

	First the glyphs are replaced: `GSUB`'s lookups for the features, in
	the order the table lists them - which is not the order of the
	features. Then each glyph gets the advance the font gives it, and
	`GPOS`'s lookups move it: pairs are set closer or apart, marks are
	put on the glyph they belong to, and the glyphs of a joined script
	are set to meet. A mark's offsets reach back over the advances
	between it and its glyph, so each glyph is drawn at the point the
	advances before it lead to, plus its offsets.

	A variable font opened away from its default (`OpenOutline`'s
	`TTA_Axes`) is shaped at that instance. A glyph's advance is
	`GlyphAdvance`'s there. A number of `GPOS` that names an item of
	`GDEF`'s store of deltas - what a single glyph or a pair is moved
	by, a point a mark or a joined glyph is attached at - takes what the
	item adds at the instance, rounded to a whole unit, a half upward:
	truetype.library's `VariationDelta` reads it (since 1.4), the reader
	its advances are varied by. At the default the store is not read.

	Where a table replaces features under conditions on the axes, the
	first set of conditions that holds at the font's instance says which
	lookups a feature has: a condition is a stretch of one axis, its
	ends included, and an axis the font does not have is at 0.

	A ligature has the lowest cluster of its parts, and the glyphs one
	glyph became all have its own. A run read from right to left is
	answered turned around.

	What a font asks for is bounded: a sequence a lookup matches is 64
	glyphs at most, lookups call each other 8 deep, and a run grows to
	64 times its length, or to 16384 glyphs where that is more. Past a
	bound the lookup in question does nothing.

	So is the time a font takes. Each table's work on the run is counted
	in pieces - a glyph a lookup comes by, a subtable looked at for it,
	a rule or a ligature tried, a glyph compared or stepped over - and a
	table has 65536 of them for each glyph of the run, a million for a
	short one. A table that has spent them does no more to the run: the
	call answers `TTS_OK` with what was done up to there. Fonts made for
	use stay far below it.

	An advance and an offset are sums, and a font can add to one as
	often as its work lasts: each stays at the largest number 32 bits
	hold, either way, and goes no further.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed; any number of tasks shape with one outline.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	`run` and its array stay the caller's. What the call allocates it
	frees before it answers.

   NOTES
	The call applies the features it is given and no others: which ones
	a script needs, and in what order its marks go, is the caller's to
	say. A mark keeps the advance the font gives it.

	Without a `GDEF` that sorts the glyphs, none is a mark or a ligature
	to a lookup's flags. Vertical advances, with what they are varied
	by, and the adjustments a font makes for single sizes in pixels are
	not read.

	An item of the store adds nothing where the store is not for the
	font's number of axes, where it has no such item, and where its row
	does not lie within the table. What an item adds is held to what a
	16-bit number holds.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`ShapeText`, truetype.library's `OpenOutline`, `GlyphIndex`,
	`FontTable`

   EXAMPLES
	    const tts = sdk.truetypeshaping;
	    var glyphs: [16]tts.ShapedGlyph = undefined;
	    for ("office", 0..) |letter, place| {
	        glyphs[place] = .{ .glyph = tb.GlyphIndex(outline, letter), .cluster = @intCast(place) };
	    }
	    var run: tts.ShapedRun = .{ .glyphs = &glyphs, .room = glyphs.len, .count = 6 };
	    const features = [_]tts.Feature{
	        .{ .tag = tts.featureTag("liga") },
	        .{ .tag = tts.featureTag("kern") },
	        .{},
	    };
	    if (sb.ShapeGlyphs(outline, tts.scriptTag("latn"), tts.TTS_LEFT_TO_RIGHT, tts.TTS_LANGUAGE_DEFAULT, &features, &run) == tts.TTS_OK) {
	        var x: i32 = 0;
	        for (glyphs[0..run.count]) |shaped| {
	            // Draw shaped.glyph at x + shaped.x_offset, shaped.y_offset.
	            x += shaped.advance;
	        }
	    }
truetypeshaping.library/ShapeText            truetypeshaping.library/ShapeText

   NAME
	ShapeText -- A text shaped by a font: the glyphs it is drawn with, each with its advance, its offsets and the place in the text it stands for.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn ShapeText(sb: *TrueTypeShapingBase, outline: *truetype.Outline, text: [*]const u8, length: u32, direction: u32, language: u32, features: ?[*]const truetypeshaping.Feature, run: *truetypeshaping.ShapedRun) i32

   SINCE
	1.1, a variable font at its instance since 1.3, Devanagari
	by its syllables since 1.5. LVO -24.

   INPUTS
	- `outline` - what truetype.library's `OpenOutline` answered.
	- `text` - the text, in UTF-8.
	- `length` - how many bytes it has.
	- `direction` - `TTS_LEFT_TO_RIGHT` or `TTS_RIGHT_TO_LEFT`: the way
	  the whole text is read.
	- `language` - the language's four letters (`languageTag("TRK ")`), or
	  `TTS_LANGUAGE_DEFAULT`.
	- `features` - features to switch on or off besides the ones a text
	  is shaped with, a list ended by a tag of 0; null for none. Their
	  `first` and `last` count the text's bytes.
	- `run` - where the glyphs go: `glyphs` with `room` saying how many
	  it holds. `count` is not read.

   RESULT
	`TTS_OK`, and `run` holds the shaped glyphs in the order they are
	drawn, from left to right, `count` of them. Otherwise:
	- `TTSERR_ROOM` - the text has more glyphs than `room`: `count` says
	  how many, and `glyphs` holds nothing of use.
	- `TTSERR_NOMEM` - there was not the memory.
	- `TTSERR_DIRECTION` - no direction of that number.
	- `TTSERR_FEATURES` - more than 31 features that hold for a part of
	  the text or whose tag is there twice.
	But for `TTSERR_ROOM`, `count` is as it was.

   BEHAVIOR
	The text is read a character at a time; a byte that is no UTF-8
	reads as U+FFFD. It is cut into stretches of one script each. A
	character many scripts share - a space, a digit, a comma - and a mark
	belong to the stretch before them, those the text starts with to its
	first stretch. Each stretch is shaped by what the font lists for its
	script: under the script's four letters in small ones (`latn`,
	`grek`, `hebr`), and for the scripts that have newer tags (`dev3`,
	then `dev2`, before `deva`) under the newest the font has. A text of shared characters
	alone is shaped by what the font lists as `DFLT`.

	Each stretch is first made the characters the font draws it with. A
	character with no mark behind it stays as it is where `GlyphIndex`
	has a glyph for it; where it has none, the character becomes its
	parts - U+00E9 an `e` and the acute U+0301 - a step at a time, as
	soon as the font has all of them. A character with marks behind it
	is taken apart as far as the font has the parts. The marks on a
	letter are then put in the order of their combining classes, those
	of one class staying as they were written, and a letter and a mark
	become the one character there is for the two where the font has a
	glyph for it: `e` and U+0301 are drawn with the font's U+00E9. A
	character the font has no form of is glyph 0. The combining grapheme
	joiner (U+034F) keeps the marks on its two sides apart: they are not
	sorted across it.

	A space of a set width the font lacks (U+2000 to U+200A, U+202F,
	U+205F, U+3000, and the no-break space U+00A0) is drawn with the
	font's space and has its width by the em - U+2003 an em, U+2009 a
	fifth of one - or by a glyph: U+2007 a digit's width, U+2008 a full
	stop's, U+202F half the space's. The non-breaking hyphen (U+2011)
	the font lacks is its hyphen (U+2010).

	Then each character becomes the glyph `GlyphIndex` has for it. Where
	a variation selector follows and `GlyphVariant` has a glyph for the
	two, that glyph stands for both, and the character and its marks
	stay as they were written.

	The features applied, where the font lists them for the script and
	the language: `rvrn`, `ccmp`, `locl`, `rlig`, `liga`, `clig`, `calt`,
	`rclt`, `curs`, `dist`, `kern`, `mark`, `mkmk`, `abvm`, `blwm`, and
	by the direction `ltra` and `ltrm`, or `rtla` and `rtlm`. One of
	them is left out by an entry in `features` of its tag and the value
	0 that holds for the whole text; an entry that holds for a part
	switches it off there. Every other entry adds its feature. What the
	language requires is applied always.

	A fraction written with the fraction slash (U+2044) is shaped as
	one: the digits before the slash get `numr`, those behind it `dnom`,
	and all of them with the slash `frac`, each where the font lists it.
	The plain `/` makes none. One of the three named in `features` for
	the whole text is the caller's alone.

	The glyphs are replaced by `GSUB` in two goes - `rvrn` first, then
	the rest - each in the order the table lists its lookups. Then the
	characters a text does not show are taken out: the soft hyphen, the
	joiners, the marks of direction, a variation selector the font had no
	glyph for. One a lookup replaced stays. Until then a sequence a
	lookup looks for steps over them, so a ligature forms across a soft
	hyphen. Two kinds stop it: the non-joiner (U+200C) ends the glyphs a
	lookup replaces, though not what it looks at before and behind
	them; and Mongolian's variation selectors (U+180B to U+180D, U+180F)
	and the tag characters (U+E0020 to U+E007F) end both.

	A stretch of Devanagari is shaped by its syllables. A syllable is
	a consonant or a vowel letter with what is written on it: the
	consonants a halant (U+094D) joins to it, a nukta, vowel signs, the
	signs from U+0900 to U+0903, the accents. In each the base is found
	- the last consonant the font has no form of below or behind
	another - and the glyphs are put in the order the font's rules are
	written for. Then the forms are asked for, a go through `GSUB`
	each, in this order: `nukt`, `akhn`, `rphf`, `rkrf`, `pref`, `blwf`,
	`abvf`, `half`, `pstf`, `vatu`, `cjct`. `rphf` holds for a ra with
	a halant that starts the syllable, `half` for the consonants before
	the base, `blwf`, `abvf` and `pstf` for those behind it, `pref` for
	one behind it that is drawn before it, the others for every glyph;
	in these goes a sequence stays within its syllable. After them the
	vowel sign written to the left of its consonants (U+093F), the ra
	that became a reph and a form drawn before the base go to where
	they are drawn, and the rest is applied in one go: `init` for such
	a vowel sign at the start of a word, `pres`, `abvs`, `blws`, `psts`,
	`haln`, and the features of any text but `liga`.

	Between a halant and the next consonant, the joiner (U+200D) asks
	for the half form and the non-joiner (U+200C) for the consonant
	with its halant. One of the forms' features switched off for the
	whole text takes its part of the syllable with it: without `rphf`
	a ra at the start stays a consonant. In such a stretch a character
	is taken apart also where the font has a glyph for it, but for
	U+0931, so that a letter with a nukta is shaped the same written as
	one character or as two; a mark keeps its advance and is not moved
	back.

	Whether a consonant has a form below or behind the base is asked of
	the font: whether `blwf`, `vatu`, `pstf` or `pref` have a rule for
	it and the halant. A font that lists the script under the older tag
	`deva` has its rules written for the halant behind the consonant:
	for it the halant behind the base is moved behind the last
	consonant. A font whose `GSUB` lists `DFLT` or `latn` and not the
	script shapes the stretch as any other text.

	Then each glyph gets the advance the font gives it, and `GPOS`
	moves it. Where `GPOS` has no `kern` feature for the language, the
	font's `kern` table is asked through `Kerning` for each glyph and
	the next one that is no mark, and the left of the two as they are
	drawn takes the value on its advance. Last, a mark's advance
	becomes 0. In a font without `GPOS`, in a text read from left to
	right, the mark is set on the glyph before it by the boxes of the
	two outlines: over the middle of the letter's box - at its left or
	right edge where the mark's combining class says so - and, for a
	class above or below, lifted or lowered until it clears the letter
	and the marks set on that side before it by a sixteenth of an em. A
	mark or a letter of no outline leaves the mark moved back by what
	its advance was.

	A variable font opened away from its default is shaped at that
	instance, as `ShapeGlyphs` says: its advances, the numbers of
	`GPOS` and the features replaced under conditions are the
	instance's. The `kern` table has no deltas, and its values are the
	same at every instance.

	What is a mark is `GDEF`'s to say. Where a font does not sort its
	glyphs, the glyph of a mark character that has no width of its own
	is one.

	A glyph's cluster is the place in the text of the first byte of its
	character. The parts a character was taken apart into all have its
	own; a letter and a mark put together have the letter's; a mark
	sorted before others and the ones it passed have the lowest among
	them. A ligature has the lowest of its parts', and the glyphs one
	glyph became all have its own. The bytes of a character taken out
	count to the glyph drawn before it - in a text read from right to
	left the one behind it in the text - and for the first glyph drawn
	to the one drawn next.

	A text read from right to left is answered turned around as a whole:
	its last stretch first.

	The bounds are `ShapeGlyphs`'s: sequences of 64 glyphs, lookups 8
	deep, a stretch growing to 64 times its length or to 16384 glyphs,
	and for each table 65536 pieces of work a glyph, a million for a
	short stretch. A table that has spent them does no more, and the
	call answers `TTS_OK` with what was done up to there. An advance
	and an offset stay at the largest number 32 bits hold.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed; any number of tasks shape with one outline.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The text, `run` and its array stay the caller's. What the call
	allocates it frees before it answers.

   NOTES
	The whole text is read in the one direction: a text of both
	directions is the caller's to cut and to order, and a bracket in a
	text read from right to left is not mirrored. Stretches are shaped
	apart, so no pair is kerned and no sequence found across the border
	of two scripts.

	Devanagari is the one script shaped by its syllables. Every other
	one is shaped by the features above and nothing is rearranged or
	told apart by its place in a word: the joined forms of Arabic and
	the syllables of the other scripts of India are not made. A
	syllable that is not whole - a vowel sign or a halant with no
	consonant before it - gets the font's dotted circle (U+25CC) for the
	missing letter, of the sign's cluster, and is shaped as a sign on
	it; in a font without that glyph it is drawn as it is written.

	Only a letter and its marks are put together: two letters that
	make one character - the single letters of a Hangul syllable - stay
	two. A run of more than 32 marks on one letter is left in the order
	it was written. In a font without `GPOS` a mark on a ligature is set
	over the whole glyph, not over the part it belongs to, and in a text
	read from right to left it is not moved at all.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`ShapeGlyphs`, truetype.library's `OpenOutline`, `GlyphIndex`,
	`GlyphVariant`, `Kerning`

   EXAMPLES
	    const tts = sdk.truetypeshaping;
	    const text = "Waffle, 1/2";
	    var glyphs: [32]tts.ShapedGlyph = undefined;
	    var run: tts.ShapedRun = .{ .glyphs = &glyphs, .room = glyphs.len };
	    // Without the usual ligatures, with old-style figures for the "1/2".
	    const features = [_]tts.Feature{
	        .{ .tag = tts.featureTag("liga"), .value = 0 },
	        .{ .tag = tts.featureTag("onum"), .first = 8, .last = 10 },
	        .{},
	    };
	    if (sb.ShapeText(outline, text.ptr, text.len, tts.TTS_LEFT_TO_RIGHT, tts.TTS_LANGUAGE_DEFAULT, &features, &run) == tts.TTS_OK) {
	        var x: i32 = 0;
	        for (glyphs[0..run.count]) |shaped| {
	            // Draw shaped.glyph at x + shaped.x_offset, shaped.y_offset;
	            // it stands for the text from byte shaped.cluster on.
	            x += shaped.advance;
	        }
	    }
